# PowerShell must reach Windows byte-for-byte. Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a
# BOM-less file as the system ANSI codepage, so a UTF-8 BOM is load-bearing —
# without it a stray multi-byte sequence becomes mojibake mid-token and the
# script fails to parse. `-text` disables line-ending normalisation so the BOM
# and the CRLFs survive a checkout on any platform.
# The scripts themselves are unmaintained (the target is Linux/WSL2); this only
# guarantees that what is in the repo still parses. See tests/test_platform.py.
*.ps1 -text

# NO LFS RULE FOR THE KIT'S ARTWORK, deliberately. `assets/plates/` is a build
# product that `scripts/ingest_kit.py` reproduces from `kit/`, and it is
# gitignored — committing it through LFS is the same mistake as committing it,
# with a bill attached. The five rules that used to sit here named
# `assets/marker/`, `assets/ballpoint/`, `assets/grease-pencil/`,
# `assets/cut-paper/` and `assets/light/`: the four ink registers and the light
# overlays of the kit before this one. Those directories have not existed since
# `862a5f9`, and a filter for a path nothing writes is a trap rather than a
# no-op — it silently LFS-ifies the day a future kit reuses one of the names.

# Sample renders and their contact sheets. Every stage re-renders these — a
# 42MB mp4 per stage is not something to keep in the pack file. Existing
# history stays as it is: rewriting it to migrate them is what destroyed the
# merge-base once already, and the win is not worth the second attempt.
samples/*.mp4 filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
samples/evidence/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
